{{also|in-kind}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|la|in specie}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|title=kind|code=103444|part of speech=n}} (section 15. “in kind”)</ref>

===Prepositional phrase===
{{head|en|prepositional phrase}}

# {{lb|en|usually after the noun}} In the form of [[goods]] and [[services]] rather than [[money]].
#: ''"How much did he give?" "Hard to say. It was all '''in kind'''."
#: ''The pay is OK, but the real attraction is all the benefits '''in kind'''.''

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|benefit in kind}}
* {{l|en|payment in kind}}
* {{l|en|grant in kind}}
* {{l|en|tax in kind}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# (paying or giving) with goods or services (as opposed to cash)
#: ''I made some donations to the charity, not in money, but '''in kind''', such as non-perishable food.''
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} In a [[reciprocal]] manner; in a similar way; in the same kind.
#* {{quote-journal|author=Alan Smith|title=Leicester City back on top as Riyad Mahrez hat-trick downs Swansea City|work={{w|The Guardian}} (London)|date=5 December 2015|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/dec/05/swansea-city-leicester-city-premier-league-match-report
|passage= Vardy drilled over after getting behind Bartley and also hit the side-netting, resulting in the loudest cheer of the day from the Swansea fans after he kicked an advertising board in frustration. He responded '''in kind''' by showing them three fingers with one hand and making a zero with the other.}}

====Usage notes====
{{wikipedia|Payment in kind}}
Frequently in the phrase ''payment '''in kind'''.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|with goods or service}} {{l|en|in trade}}
* {{sense|in a similar way}} {{l|en|equivalently}}, {{l|en|reciprocally}}, {{l|en|similarly}}

